{"name":"Nature’s Reflection","hex":"#c5d4cd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"nature","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The way things are, the totality of all things in the physical universe and their order, especially the physical world in contrast to spiritual realms and flora and fauna as distinct from human conventions, art, and technology.","The particular way someone or something is, especially\nThe essential or innate characteristics of a person or thing which will always tend to manifest, especially in contrast to specific contexts, reason, religious duty, upbringing, and personal pretense or effort."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To endow with natural qualities."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nature"},{"term":"reflection","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of reflecting or the state of being reflected.","The property of a propagated wave being thrown back from a surface (such as a mirror)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/reflection"}],"entries":[{"title":"Reflection (physics)","blocks":[{"text":"Reflection is the change in direction of a wavefront at an interface between two different media so that the wavefront returns into the medium from which it originated. Common examples include the reflection of light, sound and water waves. The law of reflection says that for specular reflection the angle at which the wave is incident on the surface equals the angle at which it is reflected."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Mount_Hood_reflected_in_Mirror_Lake%2C_Oregon.jpg/330px-Mount_Hood_reflected_in_Mirror_Lake%2C_Oregon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reflection_(physics)","wikidata":"Q165939"},{"title":"Reflection (C++)","blocks":[{"text":"Reflection in C++ refers to the usage of reflective programming, introduced to the C++ programming language in C++26. Reflection in C++, unlike other languages like Java and C#, occurs at compile time and incurs no runtime overhead."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reflection_(C%2B%2B)","wikidata":"Q141254039"},{"title":"Nature","blocks":[{"text":"Nature, in the broadest sense, is the physical world or universe. In this general sense nature refers to the laws, elements and phenomena of the physical world, including life. Although humans are part of nature, human activity or humans as a whole are often described as at times at odds, or outright separate and even superior to nature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Startrails_above_Gunung_Bromo_-_Indonesia.jpg/330px-Startrails_above_Gunung_Bromo_-_Indonesia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nature","wikidata":"Q7860"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/MIT_4.492_Daylighting%2C_Fall_2004_-_Types_of_Reflection_-_4815499473.jpg/500px-MIT_4.492_Daylighting%2C_Fall_2004_-_Types_of_Reflection_-_4815499473.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/MIT_4.492_Daylighting%2C_Fall_2004_-_Types_of_Reflection_-_4815499473.jpg/500px-MIT_4.492_Daylighting%2C_Fall_2004_-_Types_of_Reflection_-_4815499473.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sketches of specular, spread, diffuse, and compound reflection.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMIT_4.492_Daylighting%2C_Fall_2004_-_Types_of_Reflection_-_4815499473.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/F%C3%A9nyvisszaver%C5%91d%C3%A9s.jpg/500px-F%C3%A9nyvisszaver%C5%91d%C3%A9s.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/F%C3%A9nyvisszaver%C5%91d%C3%A9s.jpg/1280px-F%C